      '## 1. The Anatomy of Google AI Overviews: Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) Explained',
      'To discover **how to optimize for Google AI Overviews in 2026**, one must first understand what occurs within the millisecond-long latency between a user\'s query submission and Google\'s generative output. Google is not generating answers from a static pre-trained weights file. Doing so would produce hallucinations and outdated real-time data.',
      'Instead, Google executes a hyper-accelerated Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) workflow. When a query is initiated, Google simultaneously runs a traditional index search, gathers top organic candidate passages, vectorizes those text fragments into high-dimensional semantic space, and presents them to a specialized Gemini model instance.',
      'The generative model then evaluates candidate chunks for three strict criteria: factual consensus, distinct incremental value (Information Gain), and authoritative source reputation. The fragments that best fulfill these criteria are synthesized into cohesive prose, and their originating source URLs are appended as interactive citation chips.',

      '## 2. The Core Ranking Signals Governing AI Citations in 2026',
      'Through analyzing over 1,200 commercial search queries across competitive technology and media verticals at Cordevia Digital, our team observed five primary algorithmic signals that consistently determine citation selection:',
